srt0 — Sysl runtime crt0
========================

The arch-specific `_start` (`prog_start.s` on x86_64 / aarch64,
`syscall.asm` on TRISC) reads argc from the top of the stack and
`&argv[0]` from sp+8, then calls [[sysl_start]]. This module walks
the C-style argv, wraps each entry as a sysl `string`, and invokes
the program's `main(argv: []string)`. When main returns, srt0
exits via syscall.

C programs link against `crt0.c` instead — same ABI on the inside
(`oskit_ulib__sysl_start`), but forwards argc/argv straight to a
C `int main(int argc, char **argv)` without the `[]string` wrap.

Initial stack layout (built by PM in the child's stack region
before resume, matching the System V Application Binary Interface
process-initialization convention that Linux / NetBSD / Minix 3
also use): sp points at argc (i64), followed by argv[0..argc-1]
then NULL, then envp[] then NULL, then auxv pairs terminated by
AT_NULL, then the NUL-separated string data argv/envp point into.

    module oskit.ulib

    val ARG_MAX_ARGS = 64
    var prog_argv_strings: [64]string

    extern main(argv: []string)

    sysl_start(argc: int, cargv: **byte)
        var i = 0
        while i < argc
            if i >= ARG_MAX_ARGS
                break
            val p = cargv[i]
            var n = 0
            while p[n] != 0
                n += 1
            prog_argv_strings[i] = string(p, n)
            i += 1
        main(prog_argv_strings[:i])
        exit()
